# Current Fixed Choices for the Standings Page

This note records the metric choices now fixed for the published standings page, why they are suitable for the present project, and why other users might reasonably prefer different choices.

The purpose of this note is not to claim that these choices are uniquely correct. It is to state clearly what the page is intended to measure.

---

# 1. General Position

A standings page must choose definitions.

There is no neutral way to publish rolling sumo standings without deciding:

* what counts as a win
* which basho are in scope
* how opportunity is counted
* how totals are normalised for comparison

Different users may reasonably prefer different answers.

The current page therefore adopts one explicit and coherent model rather than pretending no choices have been made.

---

# 2. Fixed Metric Choices

The published page now uses the following basis.

## 2.1 Wins Policy: CREDITED

Wins are counted as **credited wins**.

This includes:

* fought wins
* fusensho (default wins)

It therefore reflects the official credited result rather than only bouts physically fought.

Displayed **Wins** values are standings totals under this policy.

---

## 2.2 Basho Basis: SELECTED

The user chooses a retrospective window of `N` basho (for example 5, 12, or 24).

Those basho are the standings period.

All basho in that selected period are in scope.

This basis answers questions such as:

* who has performed best over the last 5 basho?
* who has the strongest recent 2-year record?

It is a time-window basis.

---

## 2.3 Bout Basis: EXPECTED

Opportunity is counted as the number of bouts a rikishi would normally be expected to have in each selected basho, based on historical rank/division in that basho.

Currently:

* sekitori basho count as 15 expected bouts
* lower-division basho count as 7 expected bouts

Thus a rikishi who spent part of the selected period in Makushita may have a lower total Count than one who spent the whole period in Makuuchi.

---

## 2.4 Mean

Mean is the selected-window average credited wins per basho within the chosen period.

This gives a rolling average performance figure rather than a raw total alone.

---

# 3. Why These Choices Work for This Project

## 3.1 They Match the Purpose of the Site

The page is intended to be a **simple rolling standings page**, not a full research platform.

Users want quick answers to questions like:

* who is doing well recently?
* who has built momentum?
* who has strong multi-basho form?

The selected rolling window supports exactly that.

---

## 3.2 They Respect Historical Reality

Rikishi move between divisions.

A rikishi who spent one basho in Makushita did not historically have the same expected schedule as a Yokozuna.

Using expected bouts by historical basho preserves that reality instead of pretending all selected basho were identical.

---

## 3.3 They Avoid Distortion from Missing Recorded Bouts

Using expected counts rather than only available recorded bouts avoids the denominator changing merely because of absences, withdrawals, or incomplete opportunity.

This makes the Count column more principled.

---

## 3.4 They Produce One Clear Public Metric

The page needs a single default view.

Even if many alternatives are possible, a public-facing page benefits from:

* stable definitions
* repeatable outputs
* understandable columns
* no need for users to configure advanced options

These choices provide that.

---

# 4. Why These Choices May Not Work for Others

Different users may want the page to answer different questions.

---

## 4.1 Some Users Want Like-for-Like Comparisons

A user may object that:

* one rikishi had 75 expected bouts
* another had 67 expected bouts

and therefore the comparison is not equal.

That is a reasonable preference.

Such users may want:

* only rikishi with full-window expected counts
* only rikishi present in all selected basho
* only sekitori windows

The current page does not impose such filters.

---

## 4.2 Some Users Want Strength-of-Schedule Adjustment

A rikishi in lower divisions faces different opposition from one fighting sanyaku schedules.

Some users will care deeply about this.

The current page does not adjust for opponent strength.

It is a standings page, not a schedule-adjusted rating model.

---

## 4.3 Some Users Want Fought Wins Only

Some users dislike fusensho being counted equally with fought wins.

They may prefer:

* fought wins only
* separate fusensho reporting
* weighted wins

The current page uses credited wins.

---

## 4.4 Some Users Want Rates Rather Than Per-Basho Means

Some users may prefer:

* wins per expected bout
* win percentage
* efficiency measures

The current page uses a selected-basho average wins figure.

---

# 5. Why This Is Still Defensible

These objections do not show that the page is wrong.

They show that multiple legitimate metrics exist.

The present page simply chooses one coherent interpretation:

> credited recent success across the selected historical basho window, judged against historically expected opportunity.

That is a real and meaningful question.

It is not the only meaningful question.

---

# 6. Future Possibilities

If later desired, additional optional views could be added, such as:

* fought wins only
* full-window participants only
* sekitori-only comparisons
* rate-based metrics
* strength-adjusted rankings

None of these are required for the present page to be valid.

---

# 7. Final Position

The standings page now uses a clear fixed model:

* credited wins
* selected rolling basho window
* historically expected bout opportunities
* rolling average wins

This model suits the project’s goal of publishing a simple, readable, current-form standings page.

Other users may reasonably prefer other models.

That is normal.

The existence of alternatives does not weaken the legitimacy of the present one.

## On BashoBasis: Conceptually Real, Practically Niche

`BashoBasis` remains a genuine analytical distinction within the standings model.

It captures two different ways of interpreting the selected standings period:

* **SELECTED** — all basho in the chosen retrospective window count.
* **CONTAINING** — only basho within that window in which the rikishi actually appears count.

This is a legitimate modelling choice and should remain part of the internal design.

However, for most ordinary users, it is unlikely to be an important control.

---

## Why It Often Makes Little Difference

The typical user is expected to be interested in recent performance of established rikishi, especially in the upper divisions.

Examples:

* last 3 basho
* last 5 basho
* last 6 basho
* last 12 basho

For many rikishi in these cases, especially sekitori, presence is continuous across the selected period.

That means:

```text id="j0edbl"
SELECTED = CONTAINING
```

or close enough that the practical difference is negligible.

In such cases, exposing BashoBasis as a front-page user control would add complexity while changing little or nothing.

---

## Where BashoBasis Becomes Relevant

The distinction becomes more meaningful in more specialised cases.

### Long Historical Windows

Examples:

* 24 basho
* 36 basho
* 60 basho

Over long periods, a rikishi may:

* not yet have entered professional sumo
* have spent time absent
* have retired and later returned
* have only partial historical overlap with the window

In these cases, `SELECTED` and `CONTAINING` can diverge substantially.

### Lower Divisions and Rapid Movement

In lower divisions, careers are often less stable and movement more frequent.

Rikishi may enter, rise, fall, or disappear quickly, making partial-window participation more common.

Again, BashoBasis can matter.

---

## Why It Is Not an Obvious Main UI Widget

Although real, BashoBasis is not especially intuitive to casual users.

Most users naturally think in terms of:

* who is doing well recently?
* who is best in Makuuchi?
* who has momentum?

They are less likely to think in terms of denominator construction across partial historical participation.

A visible `SELECTED / CONTAINING` selector would therefore risk:

* confusing users
* cluttering the interface
* changing results only rarely
* solving a problem many users do not perceive

---

## Recommended Position

BashoBasis should remain part of the formal standings model and documented methodology.

It should be retained for analytical correctness and future flexibility.

However, it is better treated as:

* an internal modelling axis
* an expert or advanced option
* a future enhancement if justified

rather than a primary front-page control.

---

## Final View

BashoBasis is analytically legitimate but operationally niche.

It matters in some cases, especially long windows and unstable participation histories, but for the common use-case it is unlikely to matter enough to justify immediate UI prominence.

It should therefore remain in the design, while staying out of the main interface unless real demand emerges.

